# Java Best Practices

## Purpose
Capture design and engineering practices suitable for reusable Java components and applications.

## Rules

### JBP-001 · Single responsibility (SHOULD)
Organize units around cohesive responsibilities. Avoid classes that combine orchestration, persistence, networking and presentation without a clear reason.
**Verification:** architecture/code review.

### JBP-002 · Explicit contracts (MUST)
Define inputs, outputs, preconditions, failure modes and relevant side effects at module boundaries. Do not use undocumented implicit state as an integration contract.
**Verification:** API and design review.

### JBP-003 · Dependency direction (SHOULD)
Depend on abstractions when it reduces coupling, especially at infrastructure boundaries. Prefer composition over inheritance unless inheritance models a genuine substitutable relationship.
**Verification:** architecture review; dependency rules where configured.

### JBP-004 · Resource handling (MUST)
Close files, streams, network connections and other closeable resources reliably; prefer `try-with-resources` when applicable.
**Verification:** static analysis and tests.

### JBP-005 · Exceptions (MUST)
Use meaningful exception types; preserve the cause; do not swallow exceptions silently. Define recovery, propagation or translation behavior at each boundary.
**Verification:** static analysis and review.

### JBP-006 · State and mutability (SHOULD)
Prefer immutable value objects and explicit state transitions where practical. Avoid shared mutable global state.
**Verification:** review and concurrency tests if relevant.

### JBP-007 · Logging (MUST)
Log actionable operational events with useful context and appropriate severity, without disclosing secrets or unnecessary personal data. Never use logging as a substitute for error handling.
**Verification:** static analysis and review.

### JBP-008 · Cross-platform behavior (MUST when applicable)
When a project claims multiplatform support, isolate platform-specific actions behind clear interfaces and test supported platforms.
**Verification:** platform matrix and integration tests.

## Non-prescriptions
This document does not mandate a dependency-injection framework, runtime version, logging implementation or build tool. Those are project decisions, constrained by corporate policy where present.
